# AIGC 助手 — Tools & Dependencies

## Log Investigation

- Always start with timestamps and request/trace IDs to narrow scope.
- Use `rg` (ripgrep) for fast log searching; avoid `grep` on large files.
- When tailing live logs, capture to file for reproducible analysis.
- Extract structured fields (JSON logs) with `jq` for filtering and
  aggregation.

## API & Service Health

- Use `curl` or `httpie` for ad-hoc API health checks.
- Check response codes, latency headers, and error payloads.
- For model provider APIs, always check rate-limit headers
  (`x-ratelimit-remaining`, `retry-after`).

## Database & Queue

- Read-only queries for investigation — never mutate production data
  without explicit user permission.
- When querying metrics DBs (Prometheus, ClickHouse, etc.), prefer
  time-bounded queries to avoid excessive load.
- For message queues, check consumer group lag and dead-letter queues
  as first diagnostic step.

## Configuration Management

- Diff current config against last-known-good version before changes.
- Validate prompt templates for variable completeness and format.
- Track config changes in daily log with before/after snapshots.

## Python

- Use `uv` for dependency management: `uv run`, `uv pip`.

## Node.js

- Use `npm` for package management.
- Run `npm test` to verify changes when touching source or test files.

## General

- Prefer structured output (JSON, tables) over raw text for status
  reports — easier to diff and track over time.
- When running diagnostic scripts, always capture output to a file
  for later reference.
